  "query_preview": "/Through Monday/...\nIssued at 1033 PM MST Mon Nov 23 2026\n\nIsolated light snow showers will continue overnight for the higher \nterrain, with precip chances ending by early tomorrow morning as \nthe upper level shortwave and jet continue moving off to the \neast. We're already seeing some patchy fog develop along the CO/WY\nborder late this evening. Patchy fog could spread further south \nand west overnight, potentially impacting the northern I-25 \ncorridor. But fog is expected to stay north of the metro.\n\nQuiet weather is expected for much of this week. The upper level \ntrough, which moved over the area today, will sit to our east for\nthe next couple of days. Another trough will develop over the \nWest Coast by early Wednesday. In between these two systems, we'll\nhave weak ridging aloft over/just west of the area. Temperatures \nwill gradually warm through the work week, with highs reaching \nnear 60 for much of the plains by Friday. Thanksgiving looks\nrather pleasant weather-wise, with highs in the 50s across the\nplains and partly cloudy skies. Mid and upper level northwest \nwinds will strengthen on Friday, bringing moisture into the area.\nModels vary with the depth of the upper level shortwave and the \nplacement of the upper level jet. Some runs have a broader \nlongwave trough with the jet more to our north, putting us in the \nright exit region. While other runs, have a more potent shortwave\nand put us in the left exit region of the jet. The latter would \nbe much more favorable for precip and cooler temperatures in our \narea, with the most likely timing of precip sometime late Friday \nthrough early Sunday. Looking at cluster analysis, about 55% of \nthe ensemble members in the LREF bring a deeper shortwave with \nlight rain making it off the higher terrain and into the plains on\nSaturday, with 19% of the ensemble members bringing snow onto the\nplains.\n\nTemperatures and precip chances will remain highly uncertain for the \nweekend and into Monday. 25th to 75th percentile highs differ by \naround 10 to 15 degrees across the plains during this time.\nPrecipitation chances will depend heavily on what happens with \nthis first system mentioned above and a potential second shortwave\nMonday.",
